WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.243
Lab Space is a virtual science lab for families.

00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:06.445
This is the ages-four-to-six experience:

00:00:06.445 --> 00:00:09.968
simple controls, a prediction, and a shared discovery.

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:11.729
No account is needed.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.420
Starting without a prediction shows a helpful reminder.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.523
I choose Earth.

00:00:16.523 --> 00:00:18.360
A prediction is not graded:

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.668
it gives us something to compare with the observation.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.478
Both balls launch with the same speed and angle.

00:00:26.478 --> 00:00:27.811
Only gravity changes.

00:00:27.811 --> 00:00:31.364
The Moon’s weaker pull gives a longer journey.

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:35.807
The animation follows deterministic calculations, with air resistance left out.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.000
I save our observation in the notebook.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.601
It keeps the prediction, settings and result in this browser.

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:48.201
It is a local family record, not a cross-device account.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:55.698
Reopening the settings and switching to ages ten to fourteen reveals numerical speed and angle controls.

00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:59.896
Older children can change one variable and compare another run.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.296
The grown-up planner offers a gravity activity with steps and a question to discuss.

00:01:06.296 --> 00:01:08.703
The visible badge says Prewritten activity.

00:01:08.703 --> 00:01:13.516
No AI provider is enabled here, and AI never invents the physics.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:20.425
Requesting a dinosaur DNA laboratory is refused because that module does not exist.

00:01:20.425 --> 00:01:23.679
The planner stays inside the available experiments:

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:26.469
predict, try, observe, and explore again.
